</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">foremans@anl.gov</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Venkatram Vishwanath
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id7" class="ltx_text">Argonne National Laboratory</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">venkat@anl.gov</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Several variations of adaptive first-order and second-order optimization methods have been proposed to accelerate and scale the training of large language models. The performance of these optimization routines is highly sensitive to the choice of hyperparameters (HPs), which are computationally expensive to tune for large-scale models.
Maximal update parameterization <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=")" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">)</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of scaling rules which aims to make the optimal HPs independent of the model size, thereby allowing the HPs tuned on a smaller (computationally cheaper) model to be transferred to train a larger, target model.
Despite promising results for SGD and Adam, deriving <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for other optimizers is challenging because the underlying tensor programming approach is difficult to grasp. Building on recent work that introduced spectral conditions as an alternative to tensor programs, we propose a novel framework to derive <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for a broader class of optimizers, including AdamW, ADOPT, LAMB, Sophia, </span><span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Shampoo and Muon. We implement our </span><math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P derivations on multiple benchmark models and demonstrate  zero-shot learning rate transfer across increasing model width for the above optimizers. Further, we provide empirical insights into depth-scaling parameterization for these optimizers.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in generative AI, yet their performance and reproducibility depend on many interacting factors. A key aspect of training LLMs is the optimization routine, which can become unstable as models grow in size and complexity. To improve stability and efficiency, several modifications to existing optimizers have been proposed. For example, LAMB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2019</a>)</cite> proposes a layer-wise adaptive optimization routine to reduce the computational time required for training deep neural networks over large mini-batches, while Sophia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is a light-weight second-order method which achieves faster convergence than Adam and is more robust to non-convex landscapes. Muon is another recent optimizer designed explicitly for scaling with model size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jordan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernstein, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although these recent algorithms demonstrate strong performance, the computational overhead of hyperparameter (HP) tuning poses a fundamental scalability bottleneck for training LLMs. To address this challenge, practitioners have heuristically tuned HPs on smaller models to guide the search for optimal configurations in larger models. Recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Hu, 2020</a>)</cite> have formalized this approach by proposing a zero-shot HP transfer algorithm based on maximal update parameterization (<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P), which stabilizes feature learning across different model widths. <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P is implemented by carefully scaling the weights and HPs proportional to the model width, with scaling factors tailored to the specific architecture and optimization algorithm. Under <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P, feature learning is stable throughout the training process and HPs are stable across increasing model width.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the above reasons, several recent works have derived and incorporated <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for different models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thérien et al., </a>)</cite> and optimization algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blake et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ishikawa and Karakida, </a>)</cite>. Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates the increased training stability and predictability after <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P is incorporated in Sophia. Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (left) shows that the relative mean of different feature vectors remains stable across increasing model width, thereby ensuring maximal (weights not decreasing to <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>) and stable (weights not diverging) feature learning under <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P. Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (middle) demonstrates zero-shot learning rate transfer across model widths where the best validation loss is obtained at learning rate <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for all widths. Finally, Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (right) demonstrates the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“wider is always better”</span> property where the training loss improves consistently with increasing model width under <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P delivers strong results, it is tedious to implement in existing large codebases and difficult to understand in practice. To address this, authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite> proposed simpler spectral scaling conditions on the weight matrices that lead to the same width-independent and maximal feature learning properties of <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P.
This work focuses on using the more tractable spectral conditions to derive <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for a wide range of optimizers. Despite being more intuitive, using spectral conditions to derive <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P is not trivial and the analysis for each adaptive optimizer is different and requires a careful study of the order-of-magnitude of the coefficient terms that scale the gradients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our contributions are as follows: (1) we propose a general framework to derive <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P using a novel spectral scaling approach; (2) we use the proposed framework to analytically derive <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for several adaptive first and second-order optimizers (AdamW, ADOPT, LAMB, Sophia, Shampoo, Muon); (3) we implement <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for the above optimizers and validate our implementation by demonstrating zero-shot HP transfer (specifically of the optimal learning rate) across model width on benchmark LLMs (NanoGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpathy, 2022</a>)</cite>; Llama2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite> ); and (4) we provide an empirical study of zero-shot HP transfer across model depth for these optimizers to motivate future work.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S1.F1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:103.3pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20937v1/Sophia_mup_coord_check_v3_svg-raw.svg" id="S1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:131/197;" width="131" height="197"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:147.1pt;height:110.9pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S1.F1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:153.7pt;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
<object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20937v1/Sophia_val_loss_svg-raw.svg" id="S1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:203/162;" width="203" height="162"></object></span></p>
</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:143.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20937v1/Sophia_training_loss_svg-raw.svg" id="S1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:198/149;" width="198" height="149"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_centering" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P for Sophia (trained on Llama2) - Coordinate check plots for the word embedding and output logits layers (left); Zero-shot learning rate transfer across increasing model width (middle); Decreasing training loss with increasing model width (right).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The </span><math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{p}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{p}-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">norm of a vector </span><math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\real^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\real^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is defined as </span><math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{x}||_{p}:=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_{i}|^{p}\right)^{1/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{x}||_{p}:=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_{i}|^{p}\right)^{1/p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. For a matrix </span><math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\real^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\real^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}^{\alpha}=\sum_{i}\lambda_{e_{i}}^{\alpha}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}^{\alpha}=\sum_{i}\lambda_{e_{i}}^{\alpha}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> where </span><math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{e_{i}},\mathbf{u}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{e_{i}},\mathbf{u}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are the </span><math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">th eigen pair.
The spectral norm of a matrix </span><math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\real^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\real^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is defined as </span><math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="||\mathbf{A}||_{*}:=\max_{\mathbf{x}\in\real^{n}\backslash\{\mathbf{0}\}}\frac{||\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}||_{2}}{||\mathbf{x}||_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.167em" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟎</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{A}||_{*}:=\max_{\mathbf{x}\in\real^{n}\backslash\{\mathbf{0}\}}\frac{||\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}||_{2}}{||\mathbf{x}||_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and
the Frobenius norm is defined as
</span><math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{A}||_{\text{F}}:=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{m}\sum_{j=1}^{n}|\mathbf{A}_{i,j}|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">:=</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.167em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{A}||_{\text{F}}:=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{m}\sum_{j=1}^{n}|\mathbf{A}_{i,j}|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strang, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meyer, 2023</a><span id="S2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. If </span><math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denotes the rank of matrix </span><math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, then </span><math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{A}||_{*}\leq||\mathbf{A}||_{\text{F}}\leq\sqrt{r}||\mathbf{A}||_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">r</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" fence="false" mathcolor="#000000">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{A}||_{*}\leq||\mathbf{A}||_{\text{F}}\leq\sqrt{r}||\mathbf{A}||_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
If a matrix </span><math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\real^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\real^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can be written as an outer product of some vectors </span><math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\real^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\real^{m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in\real^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐯</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in\real^{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, that is, </span><math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{u}\mathbf{v}^{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮𝐯</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{u}\mathbf{v}^{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> then matrix </span><math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a rank one matrix and</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{A}||_{*}=||\mathbf{A}||_{\text{F}}=||\mathbf{u}||_{2}\cdot||\mathbf{v}||_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐯</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{A}||_{*}=||\mathbf{A}||_{\text{F}}=||\mathbf{u}||_{2}\cdot||\mathbf{v}||_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For any symmetric matrix, the spectral norm is equal to the absolute value of the maximum eigen value. Therefore, for </span><math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\real" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\real</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, for a symmetric rank one matrix </span><math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^{\text{T}}\in\real^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐮𝐮</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mtext></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^{\text{T}}\in\real^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{A}^{p}||_{*}=||\mathbf{A}||^{p}_{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐀</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{A}^{p}||_{*}=||\mathbf{A}||^{p}_{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A sequence of random vectors </span><math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{i}}\in\real^{n}\}_{i=1}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mtext></msub><msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{\text{i}}\in\real^{n}\}_{i=1}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is said to have </span><math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(n^{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(n^{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-sized coordinates if there exists constants </span><math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> such that </span><math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="An^{\alpha}\leq\sqrt{\frac{||\mathbf{x}_{\text{i}}||_{2}^{2}}{n}}\leq Bn^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">≤</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">An^{\alpha}\leq\sqrt{\frac{||\mathbf{x}_{\text{i}}||_{2}^{2}}{n}}\leq Bn^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.3.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for all </span><math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.3.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and for sufficiently large </span><math id="S2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p1.3.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In Sections </span><a href="#S3" title="3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><a href="#S4" title="4 Deriving 𝜇P using Spectral Scaling Conditions ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and Appendix </span><a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Deriving 𝜇P ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a><span id="S3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P is derived for a linear MLP trained with a batch size of </span><math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, similar to the model used in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a><span id="S3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Let us consider an MLP with </span><math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> layers. Let </span><math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\real^{n_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\real^{n_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denote the input vector and </span><math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{l}\in\real^{n_{l}\times n_{l-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{l}\in\real^{n_{l}\times n_{l-1}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denote the weight matrix for the </span><math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l-</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">th layer of the model. Then the feature vector </span><math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{l}\in\real^{n_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{l}\in\real^{n_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for the input </span><math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is given as</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}_{l}\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x}),\quad\quad\quad\quad\forall l=1,2,\ldots,L" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}_{l}\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x}),\quad\quad\quad\quad\forall l=1,2,\ldots,L</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where </span><math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{0}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{0}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Let </span><math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=g(\mathbf{h}_{L}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=g(\mathbf{h}_{L}(\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denote the loss, where </span><math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g:\real^{n_{\text{0}}}\times\real^{n_{L}}\rightarrow\real" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">:</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mtext></msub></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">×</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\real^{n_{\text{0}}}\times\real^{n_{L}}\rightarrow\real</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a loss function, </span><math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\real^{n_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\real^{n_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the target vector corresponding to the input </span><math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{L}(\mathbf{x})\in\real^{n_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{L}(\mathbf{x})\in\real^{n_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the output vector returned by the MLP. After one step of training, the change in the weight matrices is typically a function, </span><math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, of the history of the gradients. Then, the change in weights from time instant </span><math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to </span><math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can be written using the following generic update rule,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t+1)}=\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}-\eta^{(t+1)}\Psi(\{\nabla_{\mathbf{W}^{(i)}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\}_{i=1}^{t})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t+1)}=\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}-\eta^{(t+1)}\Psi(\{\nabla_{\mathbf{W}^{(i)}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\}_{i=1}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where </span><math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the learning rate at time instant </span><math id="S3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We specify the forms of </span><math id="S3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for different optimizers in Table </span><a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.p1.3.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. To reduce cumbersome notation, we omit time indices in the remaining sections unless their inclusion is necessary for clarity. This will not affect the derivation of </span><math id="S3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.3.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P as it is sufficient to analyze a single step of rule (</span><a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S3.p1.3.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) to determine the correct scaling laws </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.p1.3.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blake et al., 2025a</a><span id="S3.p1.3.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.p1.3.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Using eqs. (</span><a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.p1.3.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and (</span><a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S3.p1.3.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) the change in weights and feature vectors for any layer </span><math id="S3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.3.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, after one training step can be written as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}=-\eta\Psi(\{\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\})\;\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\;\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})=\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x})+\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x})+\mathbf{W}_{l}\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.12em;" width="1.12em"></mspace><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.12em;" width="1.12em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}=-\eta\Psi(\{\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\})\;\;\;\;\text{and}\;\;\;\;\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})=\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x})+\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x})+\mathbf{W}_{l}\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Optimizer</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Psi(\cdot)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝚿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Psi(\cdot)}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AdamW / ADOPT</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{\hat{\mathbf{m}}^{(t)}}{\sqrt{\hat{\mathbf{v}}^{(t)}}+\epsilon}+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐦</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐯</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{\hat{\mathbf{m}}^{(t)}}{\sqrt{\hat{\mathbf{v}}^{(t)}}+\epsilon}+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sophia</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{clip}\!\left(\dfrac{\mathbf{m}^{(t)}}{\max\{\gamma\mathbf{h}^{(t)},\epsilon\}},1\right)+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.428em;" width="1.428em"><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">clip</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐦</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{clip}\!\left(\dfrac{\mathbf{m}^{(t)}}{\max\{\gamma\mathbf{h}^{(t)},\epsilon\}},1\right)+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LAMB</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dfrac{\phi(||\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}||_{\text{F}})}{||\mathbf{r}_{l}^{(t)}+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}||_{\text{F}}}\left(\mathbf{r}_{l}^{(t)}+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐫</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">F</mtext></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐫</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{\phi(||\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}||_{\text{F}})}{||\mathbf{r}_{l}^{(t)}+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}||_{\text{F}}}\left(\mathbf{r}_{l}^{(t)}+\lambda\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Shampoo</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbf{L}^{(t)})^{-1/4}\;\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}}\mathcal{L}\;(\mathbf{R}^{(t)})^{-1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐋</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.447em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.280em" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐑</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{L}^{(t)})^{-1/4}\;\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(t)}}\mathcal{L}\;(\mathbf{R}^{(t)})^{-1/4}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Muon</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\mathbf{O}_{l}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐎</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\mathbf{O}_{l}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Values of <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_centering" alttext="\Psi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for different optimizers. Auxiliary variables are defined in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Deriving 𝜇P using Spectral Scaling Conditions ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Deriving 𝜇P ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Maximal Update Parametrization ( <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P )</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Authors in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Hu, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> proposed </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P to ensure that overparameterized models do not learn trivial features, or that the feature values do not blow up with increasing model width. In practice, </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P is implemented via the </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Hu, 2020</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> which ensures that the MLP weights, their initial variance and the learning rate are appropriately scaled with respect to the model width. In </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang and Hu (2020)</a></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization was introduced for MLPs where the hidden layers have the same width, that is, </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{l-1}=n_{l}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{l-1}=n_{l}=n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=2,\ldots,L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=2,\ldots,L-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. For simplicity, it was assumed that the inputs and outputs are scalars. Then, for each layer, the set of parameters </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{l},b_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}\cup\{c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∪</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{l},b_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}\cup\{c\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> comprise the </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization to</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Initialize and scale weight matrices at every layer as </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{l}=n^{-a_{l}}[\mathbf{w}_{l}^{(i,j)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐰</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{l}=n^{-a_{l}}[\mathbf{w}_{l}^{(i,j)}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, where </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{l}^{(i,j)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,n^{-2b_{l}}\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐰</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{l}^{(i,j)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,n^{-2b_{l}}\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Scale the learning rate such that </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}=-\eta\;n^{-c}\;\Psi(\{\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}=-\eta\;n^{-c}\;\Psi(\{\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where the scale of initial variance, </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and the learning rate, </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, is assumed to be width-independent.
As emphasized in Section </span><a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the theoretical principles behind </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P can be difficult to grasp. Recognizing these challenges, </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> provided the following equivalent conditions for </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}=\Theta(\sqrt{n_{l}})\quad\text{ and }\quad||\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}||_{2}=\Theta(\sqrt{n_{l}}),\quad\text{ for}\quad l=1,2,\ldots,L-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"> and </mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"> for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}=\Theta(\sqrt{n_{l}})\quad\text{ and }\quad||\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}||_{2}=\Theta(\sqrt{n_{l}}),\quad\text{ for}\quad l=1,2,\ldots,L-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.1.)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The above conditions concisely represent the requirements of </span><math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the authors futher argued that conditions (</span><a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In 3.1 Maximal Update Parametrization ( 𝜇P ) ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1.</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) can be ensured by the following </span><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">spectral scaling conditions</span><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> on the weight matrices and their one step update,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\right)\quad\text{ and }\quad||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\right),\quad\text{ for }\quad l=1,2,\ldots,L." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"> and </mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"> for </mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\right)\quad\text{ and }\quad||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\right),\quad\text{ for }\quad l=1,2,\ldots,L.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(C.2.)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The above spectral scaling conditions hold for any optimizer, and in the next section we present a framework to derive </span><math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P for any arbitrary optimizer using conditions (</span><a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In 3.2 Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2.</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Theory to practice</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">While the </span><math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P scalings in Table </span><a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Generic Framework ‣ 4 Deriving 𝜇P using Spectral Scaling Conditions ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are derived for the model described in the beginning of Section </span><a href="#S3" title="3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, empirical results in Fig. </span><a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Generic Framework ‣ 4 Deriving 𝜇P using Spectral Scaling Conditions ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and Fig. </span><a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Generic Framework ‣ 4 Deriving 𝜇P using Spectral Scaling Conditions ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> show that the derivations also hold for more practical, complex models. This section lists the assumptions required for the derived scalings to hold in practice .</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We first need to justify that deriving </span><math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P based on one time step analysis recursively yields the same scaling in the following time steps. This holds if the order of magnitude of the norms remain the same after the updates are performed, and this is formalized in Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx1" title="Assumption 1 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Note that violating Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx1" title="Assumption 1 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> will require exact cancellation which is rare to observe in practice and can be easily avoided by adding small randomness to the learning rate </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The weight updates do not cancel initial quantities.</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||\mathbf{W}_{l}+\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||\mathbf{W}_{l}+\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta(||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}+||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta(||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}+||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})+\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})+\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta(||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}+||\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta(||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}+||\Delta\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In practice, nonlinear activation functions, </span><math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, act on incoming feature vectors from the previous layer, thereby changing (</span><a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) to </span><math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}_{l}\phi(\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}_{l}\phi(\mathbf{h}_{l-1}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Our analysis directly translates to activation functions that preserve the order of magnitude of the inputs, as formalized in Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx2" title="Assumption 2 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and this phenomenon is observed for most commonly used activations which are designed to prevent the outputs from diverging or vanishing to </span><math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Additionally, Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx2" title="Assumption 2 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> also holds for most transformer layers where the activation functions are preceded by layer normalization, because the normalization maps the vectors to nonnegative constants.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">If a nonlinear activation function <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is added to each layer of the MLP, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||\phi(\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x}))||_{2}=\Theta(||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||\phi(\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x}))||_{2}=\Theta(||\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x})||_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finally, we require mild assumptions on the batch size, as stated in Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Mathematically, Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is required to ensure that the sub-multiplicative property of norms doesn’t result in a loose bound for the derivations in Section </span><a href="#S4" title="4 Deriving 𝜇P using Spectral Scaling Conditions ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to hold in practice. Intuitively, Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> holds if the update matrix </span><math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> has a low rank even for large batch sizes. We refer the reader to </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, Figure 1)</span></cite><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for empirical observations of low-rank behavior of update matrices.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The batch size, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, is fixed and independent of the width, that is, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\Theta(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\Theta(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the index of a training sample in the batch then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right\|_{2}=\Theta\left(\left\|\frac{1}{B}\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(i)}\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right\|_{2}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">‖</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐡</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right\|_{2}=\Theta\left(\left\|\frac{1}{B}\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}^{(i)}\mathbf{h}_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\right\|_{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We note that Assumption <a href="#S3.EGx3" title="Assumption 3 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> constitutes a limitation of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P as it implies a fixed batch size across model width. This is often suboptimal, as the critical batch size typically increases with model size <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">McCandlish et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. In practice, however, this can be mitigated by first tuning the smaller proxy model with a fixed batch size <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. When transferring to larger models, one can increase the batch size to improve parallelization efficiency, provided the learning rate is adjusted accordingly. Standard heuristics for this adjustment include the linear scaling rule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2017</a>)</cite> or square root scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoffer et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Deriving <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P using Spectral Scaling Conditions</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As discussed in Section </span><a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Maximal Update Parametrization ( 𝜇P ) ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a><span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, deriving </span><math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P for a particular model and optimizer boils down to determining the scaling parameters in </span><math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization, or an equivalent form. We propose a framework which only utilizes the spectral scaling conditions (</span><a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In 3.2 Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2.</span></a><span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) to derive the </span><math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization. The typical approach to derive </span><math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P is to determine the proper scaling factors for a one step gradient update, and then argue recursively that for stable input vectors under </span><math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">P, the output vectors are also stable, independent of the time (Assumption </span><a href="#S3.EGx1" title="Assumption 1 ‣ 3.3 Theory to practice ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Generic Framework</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Scaling of Model Weights and Initial Variance:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The scaling factors for the model weights and their initial variance, that is, akin to parameters </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{l},b_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{l},b_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in the </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization, can be computed by satisfying the condition on </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in (</span><a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In 3.2 Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2.</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). More rigorously, let us define the model weights as </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{l}=\sigma_{l}\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\in\real^{n_{l}\times n_{l-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{l}=\sigma_{l}\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\in\real^{n_{l}\times n_{l-1}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> where the elements of </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are sampled from some initial distribution with scaled variance, </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{-2b_{l}}\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-2b_{l}}\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. For ease of theoretical analysis, we fix </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{l}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{l}=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for all layers. Then, </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\sigma_{l}||\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}||_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\sigma_{l}||\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}||_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Since </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}||_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}||_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a random matrix with unit variance, existing results in random matrix theory can be leveraged to deduce the scaling of the spectral norm in terms of matrix dimensions </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rudelson and Vershynin, 2010</a><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vershynin (2018)</a></cite><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Then, </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can be computed by equating </span><math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{l}||\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{n_{l}/n_{l-1}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">σ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">~</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{l}||\tilde{\mathbf{W}_{l}}||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{n_{l}/n_{l-1}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Scaling of Learning Rate:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The scaling factor for the learning rate, akin to parameter </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="abc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">abc</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-parameterization, is computed by satisfying the condition on </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in (</span><a href="#S3.Ex3" title="In 3.2 Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning ‣ 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2.</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). This implies that the generic update rule in eq. (</span><a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Background ‣ Extending 𝜇P: Spectral Conditions for Feature Learning Across Optimizers" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) should be equated as,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\eta(n_{l})^{-c_{1}}(n_{l-1})^{-c_{2}}||\Psi\left(\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\right)||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐖</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub></msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">(</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||\Delta\mathbf{W}_{l}||_{*}=\eta(n_{l})^{-c_{1}}(n_{l-1})^{-c_{2}}||\Psi\left(\nabla_{\mathbf{W}_{l}}\mathcal{L}\right)||_{*}=\Theta\left(\sqrt{\frac{n_{l}}{n_{l-1}}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where the scaling constants </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are determined based on the exact nature of </span><math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
